  "summary": "Skulls in lavender: spooky made sweet, dark made wearable.",
  "description": "Pastel goth (nu-goth, creepy-cute) inverted goth's color law around 2010 on Tumblr: black platform base layers under mint, lavender and baby-pink candy colors, with skulls, bats, pentagrams and coffins rendered kawaii — bows on skeletons, glitter on gravestones. Drawing on Harajuku's fairy-kei softness and gothic lolita structure, the style spread through Lookbook and Instagram, entered mainstream fast fashion (Hot Topic to Shein), and survives as the default \"spooky-cute\" Halloween-ready dialect. Its thesis: horror and sweetness aren't opposites — pastel makes darkness gentle, and darkness makes pastel dangerous.",
  "culturalContext": "Pastel goth inverted goth's color law for the Tumblr generation: skulls in lavender spread through fashion retail worldwide, becoming the default \"spooky-cute\" dialect, and its sweet-dark thesis keeps it the gateway aesthetic between kawaii and goth communities.",
